{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310804,"nonce":"gi1Ph3K0AFV/fTekB8GVOuf6Px4=","title":"PROFILED the whole prefill (perf record, not assumption): ~23.5% is the RUST attention bmm, ~18% is pure SPIN because the Haxe SpinPool and ...","message":"Self time, dominant thread group. JIT frames are UNSYMBOLIZED so the Haxe share\ndoes not aggregate into one line — it is the remainder, corroborated\nindependently by `band_ms`.\n\n    23.54%  rayzor_tensor_bmm_threaded::{closure#0}   \u003c- ATTENTION, in RUST\n    11.99%  rayzor_tensors::worker_pool::spin_worker_loop\n     6.43%  rayzor_cpu_relax                          \u003c- (13.01% in the other group)\n     1.09%  quant::q4_k_m::quantize_block_q4_k_m\n     0.77%  __memset_avx2_unaligned_erms\n     0.68%  libm roundf   0.62% __expf_fma\n    ~50%    unresolved [JIT] addresses                \u003c- the Haxe kernels\n\nCorroboration: `NUE_PROFILE_POOL=1` gives `band_ms=6577` of `ttft=12106` = 54%\nband, **46% NOT in the band kernel**. Workers are balanced (6244-6319ms across\nw0..w7, 1.2% spread), so this is NOT load imbalance inside a dispatch.\n\nThere are **two independent thread pools**, each with spinning workers:\n  1. Haxe `SpinPool` — the Q4 band kernel (`Q4Matmul.workerCount()` = 8)\n  2. Rust `worker_pool` — `rayzor_tensor_bmm_threaded` and friends\n     (`static POOL: OnceLock\u003cWorkerPool\u003e`, rayzor-tensors/src/worker_pool.rs:756,\n     sized by `auto_kernel_threads()`)\nThey do not share workers. **While the band kernel runs, the Rust pool spins;\nwhile attention runs, the Haxe pool spins** — ~18% of samples are that spin, on\ntop of oversubscribing the box with 2xN spinning threads.\n\nA whole session of inner-loop work on the band kernel produced single digits\n(see [[perf_q4_kernel_is_spill_bound_not_source_tunable]] — six levers, all\n1-5%, four dead). This profile says the band kernel is only ~half of prefill,\nand two structural items — the Rust attention bmm (23.5%) and the two-pool\nspin (~18%) — are larger and untouched.\n\n**It also gives [[project_pure_haxe_tensor_ownership]] a MEASURED reason.**\nMoving attention off the Rust FFI is not just architectural purity: a Haxe\nattention would run on the SAME SpinPool, which removes the handoff spin as a\nside effect. That is ~18% + whatever the bmm itself gives up.\n\n1. Quantify the handoff directly: count Haxe-pool idle time during a bmm call\n   (the pool already tracks `gapEwmaUs`/`dispatches`).\n2. Either share ONE pool across both, or move attention to Haxe.\n3. `dispatches=10933` for a 2-token run is a lot; check whether fusing work per\n   dispatch shrinks the serial gaps the workers spin through.\n\n`perf record` needs `kernel.perf_event_paranoid\u003c=2` (set to 1 on the NUC).\nJIT frames are unresolved — rayzor has no perf-map emitter. Building one from\nthe `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1` `[fn-ptr] ... -\u003e 0xADDR` log (same run, sizes from\nsorted address gaps) into `/tmp/perf-\u003cpid\u003e.map` would symbolize the Haxe half\nand is the obvious next tooling step.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_prefill_profile_two_pools_idle_each_other` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}